\begin_layout Standard
Consider two spin-
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 particles,
 1 and 2,
 initially in the singlet state and moving freely in opposite directions along the z axis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A measurement of the spin of particle 1 is associated with a deterministic function 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 represents parameters of the measuring device;
 for example,
 the orientation of a Stem-Gerlach magnet.
 Another function 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 is associated with measurements on particle 2.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These functions take on the values corresponding to the two possible results of a measurement of a component of spin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)=\pm1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)=\pm1$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As usual,
 the condition of locality is assumed.
 In particular,
 the function
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 is independent of the orientation 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}$
\end_inset

 of the other measuring device and vice versa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To describe repeated measurements on identically prepared systems each 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hidden-variable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state is assumed to be associated with the pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

 which is chosen according to a probability distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\lambda\right)$
\end_inset

 is assumed normalized:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\intop_{\Lambda_{1},\Lambda_{2}}\rho\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right)d\lambda_{1}d\lambda_{2} & = & 1
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the space from which 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is chosen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 represent the pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman incorrectly states that the expectation value for the product of two individual spin measurements 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)=\pm$
\end_inset

1 in the directions 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
