\begin_layout Standard
A critical examination of the historical timeline reveals that the fundamental category error identified in this paper did not originate with modern time-tagging protocols;
 rather,
 it was baked directly into the mathematical architecture of the earliest foundational tests.
 Both the 1972 Freedman-Clauser experiment and the 1982 Aspect protocol rely on the same logical leap:
 substituting integrated macroscopic analog coincidence rates for the strict,
 event-by-event discrete joint product matrix required by John Bell’s 1964 Equation (2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 the specific mathematical definitions and physical mechanisms used to execute this substitution evolved significantly between 1972 and 1982.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The 1972 Freedman Formulation:
 Single-Channel Amplitude Attenuation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the 1972 "pile-of-plates" apparatus operated as a strict absorption filter,
 it lacked the physical capacity to record the full four-quadrant discrete joint outcome matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(A,B\in\{-1,+1\}\right)$
\end_inset

 simultaneously.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The instrumentation was physically blind to the perpendicular polarization channel (the 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 state).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To overcome this structural information loss,
 Freedman defined a continuous analog scale factor by integrating raw coincidence counts over large,
 macroscopic macro-time blocks (
\begin_inset Formula $t=100\text{ seconds}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary experimental metric was defined as the normalized coincidence ratio:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{R(\phi)}{R_{0}} & = & \frac{1}{4}\left\{ (\epsilon^{1}_{1}+\epsilon^{2}_{1})(\epsilon^{1}_{2}+\epsilon^{2}_{2})+(\epsilon^{1}_{1}-\epsilon^{2}_{1})(\epsilon^{1}_{2}-\epsilon^{2}_{2})F(0)\cos(2\phi)\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\begin_inset Formula $R(\phi)$
\end_inset

 is the integrated coincidence count rate at relative polarizer alignment 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi=\vert a-b\vert$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline reference rate measured with both polarizing plates physically removed from the optical paths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{1},\epsilon_{2}$
\end_inset

 represent continuous laboratory calibration constants tracking maximum transmittance and minimum leakage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This ratio does not represent a discrete matrix of digital switches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It maps a continuous modulation of classical light intensity dictated by Malus’s Law.
 Freedman then forced this continuous intensity function directly into a generalized CHSH inequality derived exclusively for discrete binary products—
a clear mathematical category error.
\end_layout

